Maps:
    C++ Standard Library provides several types of maps, which are associative containers that store elements formed by a combination of a key value and a mapped value. Each key value is unique and is used to identify its mapped value.
    The primary types of maps in C++ are:
    std::map:
    An ordered associative container that stores key-value pairs.
    Elements are ordered by their keys, typically implemented using a self-balancing binary search tree (like a Red-Black tree).
    Keys are unique; each key can only be associated with one value.
    Provides logarithmic time complexity for insertion, deletion, and lookup operations.
    C++

        #include <map>
        #include <string>

        std::map<std::string, int> studentGrades;
        studentGrades["Alice"] = 95;
        studentGrades["Bob"] = 88;
    std::unordered_map:
    An unordered associative container that stores key-value pairs.
    Elements are not ordered by their keys; instead, they are organized into buckets based on hash values of the keys.
    Keys are unique.
    Provides average constant-time complexity for insertion, deletion, and lookup, but worst-case linear time complexity (due to hash collisions).
    C++

        #include <unordered_map>
        #include <string>

        std::unordered_map<std::string, int> userSessions;
        userSessions["user123"] = 1001;
        userSessions["user456"] = 1002;
    std::multimap:
    Similar to std::map, it is an ordered associative container.
    The key difference is that it allows multiple elements to have the same key.
    Elements are ordered by their keys.
    C++

        #include <map>
        #include <string>

        std::multimap<std::string, std::string> contactNumbers;
        contactNumbers.insert({"John Doe", "123-456-7890"});
        contactNumbers.insert({"John Doe", "987-654-3210"});
    std::unordered_multimap:
    Similar to std::unordered_map, it is an unordered associative container.
    Allows multiple elements to have the same key.
    Elements are not ordered by their keys.
    C++

        #include <unordered_map>
        #include <string>

        std::unordered_multimap<std::string, std::string> blogPostTags;
        blogPostTags.insert({"C++", "Introduction to C++"});
        blogPostTags.insert({"C++", "Advanced C++ Concepts"});
    In summary, the choice between these map types depends on whether you require elements to be ordered by keys (std::map, std::multimap) and whether you need to allow duplicate keys (std::multimap, std::unordered_multimap). std::unordered_map and std::unordered_multimap generally offer faster average-case performance due to their hash-table implementation.




        To use maps in C++, follow these steps: include the <map> header.
    C++

        #include <map>
    declare a map.
    The general syntax for declaring a map is std::map<KeyType, ValueType> mapName;. 
    KeyType: The data type of the keys.
    ValueType: The data type of the values associated with the keys.
    mapName: The name you give to your map instance.
    Example:
    C++

        std::map<std::string, int> ages; // Map with string keys and int values
        std::map<int, double> scores;   // Map with int keys and double values
    Initialize and Insert Elements.
    You can initialize a map at declaration or insert elements later. Initialization at declaration.
    C++

            std::map<std::string, int> people = { {"John", 30}, {"Jane", 25}, {"Mike", 35} };
    Inserting elements using square brackets ([]).
    C++

            std::map<std::string, int> ages;
            ages["Alice"] = 28;
            ages["Bob"] = 32;
    Inserting elements using insert() function.
    C++

            std::map<std::string, int> ages;
            ages.insert({"Charlie", 40});
            ages.insert(std::make_pair("Diana", 22)); // Using std::make_pair
    Access Elements.
    Access elements using their keys inside square brackets.
    C++

        std::cout << people["John"] << std::endl; // Output: 30
    If you try to access a non-existent key using [], it will insert that key with a default-constructed value. Iterate Through a Map.
    You can iterate through a map using a range-based for loop or iterators.
    Range-based for loop (C++11 and later):
    C++

            for (const auto& pair : people) {
                std::cout << pair.first << ": " << pair.second << std::endl;
            }
    using iterators.
    C++

            for (auto it = people.begin(); it != people.end(); ++it) {
                std::cout << it->first << ": " << it->second << std::endl;
            }
    Find Elements.
    Use the find() method to search for a key. It returns an iterator to the element if found, or mapName.end() if not found.
    C++

        auto it = people.find("Jane");
        if (it != people.end()) {
            std::cout << "Jane's age is: " << it->second << std::endl;
        } else {
            std::cout << "Jane not found." << std::endl;
        }
    Remove Elements.
    Use the erase() method to remove elements by key or by iterator.
    C++

        people.erase("Mike"); // Remove by key
        people.erase(it);     // Remove by iterator
        people.clear();       // Remove all elements


