The C++ Standard Template Library (STL) offers a rich set of components frequently used in Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA) for efficient problem-solving. These components can be broadly categorized into Containers, Iterators, and Algorithms.
1. Containers: These are classes that store collections of objects, representing various data structures.
Sequence Containers:
std::vector: Dynamically sized arrays, offering efficient random access and fast appends/removals at the end. Widely used for general-purpose storage.
std::list: Doubly-linked lists, providing efficient insertions and deletions anywhere in the list but slower random access.
std::deque: Double-ended queues, allowing efficient insertions and deletions at both ends.
std::array: Fixed-size arrays with compile-time size, offering performance similar to C-style arrays but with bounds checking and other benefits.
Associative Containers:
std::set: Stores unique elements in a sorted order, based on a key. Efficient for searching, insertion, and deletion.
std::map: Stores key-value pairs in a sorted order based on the key. Efficient for lookup, insertion, and deletion by key.
std::multiset: Similar to std::set but allows duplicate elements.
std::multimap: Similar to std::map but allows duplicate keys.
Unordered Associative Containers:
std::unordered_set: Stores unique elements in an unsorted manner using hash tables, providing average O(1) complexity for most operations.
std::unordered_map: Stores key-value pairs in an unsorted manner using hash tables, providing average O(1) complexity for most operations.
std::unordered_multiset: Similar to std::unordered_set but allows duplicate elements.
std::unordered_multimap: Similar to std::unordered_map but allows duplicate keys.
Container Adapters: These provide a specific interface to underlying containers.
std::stack: LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) data structure.
std::queue: FIFO (First-In, First-Out) data structure.
std::priority_queue: A queue where elements are retrieved based on their priority.
2. Iterators: These act as generalized pointers, providing a way to access elements within containers. They enable traversal and manipulation of container elements in a generic manner, allowing algorithms to work with different container types.
3. Algorithms: These are a collection of generic functions that operate on ranges of elements defined by iterators. They provide common operations like:
Sorting: std::sort, std::stable_sort, std::partial_sort.
Searching: std::find, std::binary_search, std::lower_bound, std::upper_bound.
Manipulation: std::copy, std::fill, std::remove, std::unique, std::reverse.
Numeric Operations: std::accumulate, std::iota.
These STL components significantly streamline DSA implementations in C++ by providing optimized, ready-to-use data structures and algorithms, allowing developers to focus on problem-solving logic rather than low-level implementations.
